    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Environmental policy in Iran links a constitutional commitment to environmental protection with practical questions about water use, pollution, land degradation, and ecosystem conservation. Groundwater research and wetland programmes illustrate the importance of distinguishing legal commitments, management interventions, and measured environmental outcomes. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": [
      "Environmental policy",
      "Groundwater",
      "Wetlands",
      "Water management",
      "Conservation",
      "Public policy"
    ],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T00:10:10Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T00:10:10Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Environmental Policy in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran's environmental commitments, groundwater constraints, wetland conservation programmes, and the evidence needed to assess policy outcomes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "legal-commitment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Environmental protection as a public responsibility",
        "content": "Article 50 of Iran's constitution frames environmental protection as a public duty and prohibits economic activity associated with pollution or irreparable environmental destruction. A World Bank environmental assessment published in 2005 reproduced that commitment while identifying deterioration in water, air, land, and forest resources. Its central distinction remains useful for reading policy documents: a legal obligation and an environmental outcome are different kinds of evidence. The report's diagnosis is historical, not a measurement of conditions at the current cutoff. [s1]\n\nThe assessment organized environmental costs across water, air, land resources, forests, and coastal areas. This broad scope helps explain why environmental policy extends beyond protected landscapes. Urban pollution, agricultural land management, wastewater, and coastal ecosystems can all fall within its concerns. The report's old cost estimates are not presented here as current losses, and no monetary values have been updated without supporting data. [s1]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "groundwater-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why falling extraction can signal worsening scarcity",
        "content": "A peer-reviewed study by Noori and colleagues examined national groundwater records for 2002–2015. It found declining groundwater quantity and deteriorating quality across much of Iran. Crucially, lower annual extraction did not necessarily demonstrate successful conservation: the authors identified depletion and salinization as major constraints on obtaining fresh groundwater. [s2]\n\nThe study also distinguished withdrawal from consumption and examined regional variation. This matters when interpreting irrigation improvements: reducing water applied at a field does not automatically establish an equivalent improvement in basin water balance. Return flows and recharge must also be considered. The endpoint comparison below is historical and cannot establish the trajectory after 2015. [s2]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "groundwater-endpoints",
        "type": "table",
        "content": {
          "title": "Reported national groundwater withdrawal: historical endpoints",
          "dataset_id": "groundwater-withdrawal-endpoints"
        },
        "source_ids": [
          "s2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "wetland-management",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Wetlands connect conservation with livelihoods",
        "content": "Wetland restoration provides a concrete example of policy crossing institutional and geographic boundaries. UNDP's May 2025 factsheet describes the Conservation of Iranian Wetlands Project as combining basin management, sustainable agriculture, biodiversity conservation, and alternative livelihoods. It identifies the Department of Environment and Ministry of Agriculture Jihad among its implementing partners, alongside Japanese support. [s3]\n\nThe factsheet lists work at Lake Urmia, Shadegan, Bakhtegan, Parishan, and Anzali. These are distinct project locations; their inclusion does not imply identical environmental conditions or recovery. It also identifies weak enforcement, pollution, and insufficient coordination between organizations as obstacles. This institutional account is useful evidence about programme design and recognized challenges, but its reported achievements need independent evaluation. [s3]\n\nIn December 2024, UNDP announced further Japanese support for wetland management and climate resilience. The announcement described planned cooperation with the Department of Environment and the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, or FAO, extending to 2028. Its emphasis on water-efficient agriculture and alternative livelihoods shows the intended connection between conservation and local economic activity. An announced implementation period does not demonstrate that restoration has been completed. [s4]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s3",
          "s4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "judging-outcomes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Assessing results at the right scale",
        "content": "These examples point to a practical standard for evaluating environmental policy: compare interventions with conditions in the relevant ecosystem. A project may document training or changed farming practices while the wider basin remains under pressure. Conversely, a national average can conceal different local conditions. Groundwater research and wetland programme documents therefore need to be read together, with their different methods and reporting periods made explicit. Neither a project announcement nor a historical national trend alone establishes present environmental recovery. [s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s2",
          "s3",
          "s4"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [
      {
        "id": "groundwater-withdrawal-endpoints",
        "title": "Iran: annual groundwater withdrawal, selected historical endpoints",
        "geography": "Iran, national aggregate",
        "population_scope": "Groundwater extraction from deep wells, semideep wells, qanats, and springs covered by the study's national water-resources records",
